---
import { phones, address, facebook } from "../data/contact";
import { offerPages } from "../data/navigation";
import { versionLabel, buildStamp } from "../data/version";

/**
 * A name, four link columns and a rule.
 *
 * Structure fixed here: the column labels are headings rather than paragraphs styled to
 * look like headings, the link columns are real `<nav>`s with lists in them, and the loose
 * `<a>` elements in the last column are list items. Before this, a screen reader reaching
 * the footer found four unnamed groups of text with links scattered through them.
 *
 * The site name is a paragraph, not a heading: the footer repeats the masthead, and the
 * masthead is identity rather than a section of the document. It also stands outside the
 * column grid rather than being the first of its tracks - see `.footer__columns` for why.
 *
 * Since 1.0 this carries the flat list the menu no longer offers: the three categories moved
 * into an "Oferta" panel, which put them one click further from every page. The offer column
 * here is not decoration, and it is not a shorter version of the menu - it is what pays that
 * cost back, together with the "Pozostałe grupy" nav at the foot of every category page. Do
 * not trim it to save a line, and keep it derived from `offerPages` so it cannot fall out of
 * step with the panel.
 *
 * **It is no longer a list of every page.** "Inspiracje" stood in this column until September
 * 2026, when the owners said that inspirations are not part of the offer and asked for it to
 * come out of the footer rather than move to another column. So the slideshow is now reachable
 * from the menu and the home page, and from nowhere down here - see `navigation.ts` and
 * docs/inwentaryzacja.md. Everything above about the offer column still holds.
 *
 * **Why "Informacje" became two columns.** It carried six entries against three, two and two
 * elsewhere, so it was half again as tall as any other column. The split is not cosmetic
 * either: it names a pairing this comment already argued for. "Polityka prywatności" and
 * "Ustawienia mapy" belong together, because the consent bar asks about data processing and
 * the policy is the document it asks on behalf of; "Prywatność" is that pair's label.
 * "Prywatność" is our word and not the owners' - it is on their review list in
 * docs/inwentaryzacja.md.
 *
 * **Facebook stays in "Informacje", and that is the owners' second word on it.** The split
 * first moved it up under the two phone numbers, on the argument that it is a third way of
 * reaching the holding rather than a page of the site; they saw that and asked for it back.
 * So "Gospodarstwo" is the address and the phones - the ways to reach a person - and the link
 * to somebody else's platform sits with the rest of the site's destinations. Do not move it
 * again without asking.
 *
 * With JavaScript off `.footer__resetRow` is hidden and "Prywatność" holds a single link.
 * That is correct rather than a regression: there was never any consent to withdraw.
 *
 * **Every link is underlined, and that is a fix rather than a flourish.** `--footer-link`
 * #E4E7DC against `--footer-body` #C3CBB8 is 1.34:1, so colour alone could not tell a link
 * from the text around it - the 3:1 WCAG 1.4.1 asks for is not close. It showed worst in the
 * contact column, where tappable phone numbers stood directly under an untappable address at
 * the same size; `/kontakt/` has underlined those same numbers all along (`Contact.astro`).
 * Until this change the one underlined thing down here was `.footer__reset`, which is the one
 * thing that is not a link.
 *
 * Two departures from the prototype, both deliberate and both kept:
 *
 * - "Polityka prywatności" is here, and the prototype's dead `#` is not what put it here.
 *   It stood empty until the page existed, because an entry that goes nowhere is worse than
 *   one that is absent; `/polityka-prywatnosci/` arrived in September 2026 and the link went
 *   in at the same moment, together with the two in `ConsentBanner` and `MapEmbed`.
 *   **It is also the only page on the site that is not in the menu.** That is deliberate -
 *   `navigation.ts` feeds `allPages`, and a legal document does not belong in the 404's list
 *   of "where you were probably going" - so this column is the only route to it from most of
 *   the site. Do not trim it away as a duplicate of the menu; it is not in the menu.
 * - "Ustawienia mapy" is here and is not in the design. Withdrawing consent has to be as
 *   easy as giving it, and this is the only place a visitor can reach it from. It is
 *   hidden without JavaScript, where there was never any consent to withdraw.
 */
const year = new Date().getFullYear();
---

<footer class="footer">
  <div class="footer__brand">
    <p class="footer__name">Gospodarstwo Ogrodnicze „Saran”</p>
    <p class="footer__blurb">
      Sprzedaż kwiatów balkonowych, rabatowych, wieloletnich i chryzantem wielkokwiatowych.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="footer__columns">
    <nav class="footer__col" aria-labelledby="footer-oferta">
      <h2 class="footer__label" id="footer-oferta">Oferta</h2>
      <ul class="footer__list">
        {
          offerPages.map((item) => (
            <li>
              <a class="footer__link" href={item.href}>
                {item.label}
              </a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <div class="footer__col">
      <h2 class="footer__label">Gospodarstwo</h2>
      <p class="footer__text">{address.street}<br />{address.postalCode} {address.city}</p>
      <ul class="footer__list">
        {
          phones.map((phone) => (
            <li>
              <a class="footer__link" href={phone.href}>
                {phone.person} {phone.display}
              </a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </div>

    <nav class="footer__col" aria-labelledby="footer-info">
      <h2 class="footer__label" id="footer-info">Informacje</h2>
      <ul class="footer__list">
        <li><a class="footer__link" href="/o-nas/">O nas</a></li>
        <li><a class="footer__link" href="/faq/">FAQ</a></li>
        <li><a class="footer__link" href="/kontakt/">Kontakt i dojazd</a></li>
        <li>
          <a class="footer__link" href={facebook} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Facebook</a>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <nav class="footer__col" aria-labelledby="footer-prywatnosc">
      <h2 class="footer__label" id="footer-prywatnosc">Prywatność</h2>
      <ul class="footer__list">
        <li>
          <a class="footer__link" href="/polityka-prywatnosci/">Polityka prywatności</a>
        </li>
        <li class="footer__resetRow">
          <button class="footer__link footer__reset" type="button" data-consent-reset>
            Ustawienia mapy
          </button>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
  </div>

  <div class="footer__bar">
    <span>© {year} Gospodarstwo Ogrodnicze „Saran”</span>
    <span class="footer__version" title={buildStamp}>{versionLabel}</span>
  </div>
</footer>

<style>
  .footer {
    background: var(--ink);
    color: var(--footer-body);
    /* The default focus outline is --green (#5A6D46), and that is 2.63:1 on --ink - below
       the 3:1 WCAG 1.4.11 asks of a focus indicator. A keyboard passing through the footer
       could not see where it was. --green-lit gives 9.49:1. */
    --focus-ring: var(--green-lit);
    /* The link underline, remapped for this ground the way Compositions.astro remaps the
       same token for its dark variant. The value differs from that one (40% against 35%)
       because the ground does: 40% composited on --ink #1F2A21 resolves to #727970, which
       is 3.32:1 - over the 3:1 WCAG 1.4.11 asks of an indicator. 35% would give 2.86:1. */
    --rule-link: rgb(238 240 230 / 40%);
    padding: var(--pad-footer) var(--edge);
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }

  /* Four link columns, with the name and blurb lifted out of the grid into `.footer__brand`
     above. The handoff has the name as the first of four columns and that is how this stood
     until the "Informacje" split: five equal tracks squeezed the name to 208px, where
     "Gospodarstwo Ogrodnicze „Saran”" breaks over three lines. The owners reported it, and
     an auto-fit grid has no way to make one track wider than the rest - `grid-column: span 2`
     would invent an implicit second column at the single-column widths, and a fixed first
     track is the media query this project does not use. Taking the name out of the grid is
     the move that costs nothing: it gets the full width and sets on one line, and the four
     link columns get the room back.

     170px rather than the handoff's 220px, and the value is measured rather than picked: the
     widest thing in this footer is the phone row "Mateusz 722 238 987" at 154px, so 170px is
     the smallest round floor that never wraps one. It also decides how long four columns
     survive. Four tracks need 4x170 plus three 2.5rem gaps = 800px of content, which the
     --edge formula reaches at about 889px of viewport - so 1024, 1152, 1200 and up all hold
     four columns with nothing orphaned underneath. 220px would have needed 1000px and left
     1024 with three columns and a lone "Prywatność". Below that: ~768px gives three columns,
     ~620px two, and under ~420px `min(100%, 170px)` gives one.

     The row gap is 2.25rem against the handoff's 1.5rem, for the widths where a column does
     wrap: 1.5rem against the 0.35rem inside a column did not read as a break between two
     rows of columns, it read as one long run. */
  .footer__columns {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 170px), 1fr));
    gap: 2.25rem 2.5rem;
  }

  .footer__brand {
    display: grid;
    justify-items: start;
  }

  .footer__col {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.35rem;
    align-content: start;
    justify-items: start;
  }

  .footer__name {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    color: var(--bone);
  }

  /* The header's tagline used to stand here, one sentence above a blurb that is a superset
     of it: "Sprzedaż kwiatów balkonowych, rabatowych i chryzantem" over "Sprzedaż kwiatów
     balkonowych, rabatowych, wieloletnich i chryzantem wielkokwiatowych". It went back to
     the header, where the specification puts it; the column keeps the blurb, which says
     more. */

  /* 44ch, not the handoff's 34ch. 34ch was a cap inside a ~270px column, where it never bound
     on anything; in the full-width brand row it does, and it broke the sentence over three
     lines under a name that sets in one. 44ch measures 396px against the name's 386px, so the
     two end together and the sentence sits on two lines. */
  .footer__blurb {
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    max-width: 44ch;
    /* 0.35rem, not 0.15rem: the blurb now sits directly under the name rather than under
       the tagline. */
    margin-top: 0.35rem;
  }

  /* A heading now, with the type the label had. */
  .footer__label {
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 0.64rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--footer-label);
  }

  .footer__text {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  /* No gap and no margin of its own: the sized rows below carry both. A 0.95rem line box at
     the global 1.68 line-height is 25.5px, so every row centres its text inside its own
     half-leading, and the column's 0.35rem is all the separation the label needs. A `gap`
     on top would only stretch the column. */
  .footer__list {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0;
    justify-items: start;
  }

  /* `inline-flex` + a floor on the height, the same shape .cta-line and .skip in global.css
     use. `justify-items: start` above keeps each box as wide as its text, so the target is
     the link and not the whole column.

     **32px, not the `var(--tap)` 44px this project asks for everywhere else**, and that is
     the owners' call after seeing both. 44px is what the rule says and what shipped first;
     they reported the columns as too airy, and the measurement says why: the rows used to sit
     on a 32px pitch (a 29px line box plus 0.2rem), so 44px stretched the rhythm by half again.
     32px puts the pitch back exactly where it was while still growing the target, because
     before this the tappable box was the ~18px inline box of the `<a>` and not the 29px row
     around it. It keeps WCAG 2.2 AA: 2.5.8 wants 24x24, and the pitch clears it with room.
     What it gives up is 2.5.5, the AAA-level 44px the rest of the site holds to - which is
     why this is the one place in the project that names a number instead of using --tap.

     The underline is the affordance: --footer-link against --footer-body is 1.34:1, so
     colour alone was not telling a link from the text beside it. See the component header. */
  .footer__link {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--footer-link);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
    text-decoration-color: var(--rule-link);
    text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 32px;
  }
  .footer__link:hover {
    color: var(--paper);
    text-decoration-thickness: 2px;
  }

  /* A button, because it changes stored state rather than going anywhere - dressed as a
     link so the column reads as one list. Its underline now comes from .footer__link with
     every other row; it used to be written here, which made the one item in the footer that
     is not a link the only one that looked like one. */
  .footer__resetRow {
    display: none;
  }
  :global(html.js) .footer__resetRow {
    display: block;
  }

  .footer__reset {
    font-family: inherit;
    background: none;
    border: 0;
    padding: 0;
    text-align: start;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .footer__bar {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem 2rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--footer-rule);
    padding-top: 1rem;
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    color: var(--footer-label);
  }

  .footer__version {
    margin-inline-start: auto;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
</style>
